%N A398129 Primes in the conjectured unique infinite branch of the tree of primes of the form 6k-1 defined in the comments.
%C A398129 According to a stronger version of Goldbach's conjecture, any prime n>3 is the middle term between two primes p and q such that p<n<q, n=p+d=q-d, or p+q=2n. Example: 3<5<7 or 3<7<11.
%C A398129 Choose p and q such that d>0 is minimized. Then we construct a rooted tree with root 3 and parent(n)=p. The tree is composed of two subtrees: the primes of form 6k-1 and those of form 6k+1, and each subtree is conjectured to have only one infinite branch.
%H A398129 Andrew Howroyd, <a href="/A398129/b398129.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A398129 Andrew Howroyd, <a href="/A398129/a398129.txt">PARI Program</a>, 2026.
%H A398129 Mauro Torelli, <a href="/A398129/a398129.jpg">A tree of primes</a>.
%e A398129 23 is a prime of form 6k-1, but no prime has it as a parent. So it is a leaf in the tree, and surely not in the infinite branch. 113 has parent 89, but the branch starting with 113 ends with 191, so these primes are not in the sequence.
